CSS Selectors:
CSS Selectors are the different patterns or methods which are used to target and style specific HTML elements within a web page. 
These are of 4 types.

i) Element selectors: the html element which needs to be styled is used, followed by the curly braces having the CSS properties. It targets a number of same HTML
elements together.
Ex: To target all the h2 headings together and style them with same CSS properties.
    h2{
      color: blue;
      font-size: larger;
    }

ii) Class selector: it is used to group a number of same or differnet HTML elements. All such elements are given a same class attribute name and all of them can be
target and styled in one go. The (.) dot followed by the class name is used to provide CSS. 
  .divClass{
    margin:10px;
    padding:20px;
    
iii) id selector: it is used to target a single HTML element as ID is used to uniquely identify a HTML element. '#' followed by the id name is used to target and
style such an element.
    #mainHeading{
      font-size: 30px;
      font-family: sans-serif;
    }
